end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.5 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the state vector ${x}_{1},$ we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.2 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.5 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ 0.43 \\\\ 0.28\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-135442/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4821de45-1b6b-5b4f-be0e-96cf5395bc8d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-135442"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-35846"] :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the state vector ${x}_{2},$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0},\\,{x}_{2}=P{x}_{1}\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ 0.71\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ 0.71\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.413 \\\\ 0.587\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-135450/selection/b7ceef5cbf942992d17e146008798d09bba36b4bdefbdecdce3794352eb3db45" :answer/id #uuid "7301decc-e4cb-5420-b963-05c7c9305215" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.434 \\\\ 0.566\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135450/selection/e9699b8267a362eea31a4bca894a2596646fefbee190567a1fc0d2c5769e8ee2" :answer/id #uuid "d8505ec2-14fa-5c0a-81e2-7f7c097f6c59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.364 \\\\ 0.636\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135450/selection/301e0e5fc48bcb3598572bbcb3186208dab6c491bdc234aee520b613ba302e38" :answer/id #uuid "6f9ebbd8-3581-55bc-a6a2-1601eb5fe0d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.324 \\\\ 0.676\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135450/selection/2b67064bd0fb8ba79618bf82df073a3daf7cd234e1bb00f64e276b4a624b049c" :answer/id #uuid "93012f05-6416-511b-b5f4-05767a8f7dad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.312 \\\\ 0.688\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135450/selection/f92d2c90f149eaa42cf8aece903a50b4b50848815e2fc889a4a82933f68485b2" :answer/id #uuid "fb79c0ad-eae2-57e6-a5e0-02d3f28927ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0.296 \\\\ 0.704\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135450/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "12f641fe-2871-5149-bfe0-0cb7ec41d57c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-135450/selection/301e0e5fc48bcb3598572bbcb3186208dab6c491bdc234aee520b613ba302e38" :answer-field/choices ["q-135450/selection/b7ceef5cbf942992d17e146008798d09bba36b4bdefbdecdce3794352eb3db45" "q-135450/selection/e9699b8267a362eea31a4bca894a2596646fefbee190567a1fc0d2c5769e8ee2" "q-135450/selection/301e0e5fc48bcb3598572bbcb3186208dab6c491bdc234aee520b613ba302e38" "q-135450/selection/2b67064bd0fb8ba79618bf82df073a3daf7cd234e1bb00f64e276b4a624b049c" "q-135450/selection/f92d2c90f149eaa42cf8aece903a50b4b50848815e2fc889a4a82933f68485b2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-135450" :question/id #uuid "b6f2bb21-8c66-5d1d-89c2-970e35eeb5ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-135450" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.6\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the state vector ${x}_{2},$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0},\\,{x}_{2}=P{x}_{1}\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.38 \\\\ 0.62\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.38 \\\\ 0.62\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.324 \\\\ 0.676\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-135450/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4821de45-1b6b-5b4f-be0e-96cf5395bc8d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-135450"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-35845"] :question/problem "A certain company produces two types of smartphones: Phone $A$ and Phone $B.$ The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of smartphones each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Phone $A$** | **Phone $B$** |\n| To: | **Phone $A$** | $0.6$ | $0.3$ |\n| **Phone $B$** | $0.4$ | $0.7$ |  |\n\nThat is, $40%$ of Phone $A$ customers switch to Phone $B$ each year, and $30%$ of Phone $B$ customers switch to Phone $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600\\,000$ customers use Phone $A,$ while $400\\,000$ customers use Phone $B.$ How many customers will use Phone $A$ in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each smartphone is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}600\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}600\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.48 \\\\ 0.52\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $48%$ of customers will use Phone $A.$ Computing $48%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.48=480\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $480\\,000$ customers will use Phone $A$ in one year." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-135466/selection/fa2764e4d443b0fd1a8b9b79f38b985d2b576f72681c3b867c06cfeb24c839e4" :answer/id #uuid "12f265fc-3789-5bc9-8fce-070d1d0bbf98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "360" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135466/selection/6d5e653a0cc5c6e6fc44c5c3386727c89009586dc5171332dccb4c794ca30987" :answer/id #uuid "86f475a4-9810-5305-a243-fbd7527ce134" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "330" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135466/selection/1504f6a2db112b12bec365018b71356834e3270976cc2abc3301499336481fb4" :answer/id #uuid "57f41175-8631-53ca-905d-1aa815f840bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "290" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135466/selection/de27e7d5dcadf9c81d8939267dcde4b168b34b504001cdbb7b6c04af0b060022" :answer/id #uuid "d0d6ac7b-eeb0-54b6-b3aa-2f177bb4c9f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "310" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135466/selection/422f38974dca0bcd373b73d07f2017a88ccdf0a9447fd9174a576587603d572e" :answer/id #uuid "8e53e619-5d0d-5c44-80e7-cc6eb47f044a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "420" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135466/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d09405d1-0c7d-5393-affa-682c82829d3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-135466/selection/6d5e653a0cc5c6e6fc44c5c3386727c89009586dc5171332dccb4c794ca30987" :answer-field/choices ["q-135466/selection/fa2764e4d443b0fd1a8b9b79f38b985d2b576f72681c3b867c06cfeb24c839e4" "q-135466/selection/6d5e653a0cc5c6e6fc44c5c3386727c89009586dc5171332dccb4c794ca30987" "q-135466/selection/1504f6a2db112b12bec365018b71356834e3270976cc2abc3301499336481fb4" "q-135466/selection/de27e7d5dcadf9c81d8939267dcde4b168b34b504001cdbb7b6c04af0b060022" "q-135466/selection/422f38974dca0bcd373b73d07f2017a88ccdf0a9447fd9174a576587603d572e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-135466" :question/id #uuid "675dc848-9bb3-55cc-acc9-5f6cc9b0d9c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-135466" :question/problem "A certain cinema sells three types of popcorn: traditional, butter, and caramelized. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of popcorn each month:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Traditional** | **Butter** | **Caramelized** |\n| To: | **Traditional** | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| **Butter** | $0.3$ | $0.5$ | $0.2$ |  |\n| **Caramelized** | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |  |\n\nFor example, $30%$ of traditional popcorn customers switch to butter popcorn each month, and $40%$ of butter popcorn customers switch to caramelized popcorn.\n\nCurrently, $400$ customers buy traditional popcorn, $300$ customers buy butter popcorn, and $300$ customers buy caramelized popcorn. How many customers will purchase butter popcorn in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of popcorn customers is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}400 \\\\ 300 \\\\ 300\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}400 \\\\ 300 \\\\ 300\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.3 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.1 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.5 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.3 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.25 \\\\ 0.33 \\\\ 0.42\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $33%$ of customers will buy butter popcorn. Computing $33%$ of $1\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\cdot 0.33=330.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $330$ customers will buy butter popcorn in one month." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-135466/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e93dde4-a8e8-548a-801e-3d9a320a4513"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-135466"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-135465/selection/704aec2380857c58272f76dc457192d5f0618fec55c4bc6c2c0c2c61accea4ea" :answer/id #uuid "f3ffd6c5-f462-513c-a984-0b636995dc1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "470\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135465/selection/a9c70ee39590074bf42ecc3e38718cc08ac45916790a55fc58f014f0fb921912" :answer/id #uuid "3a881244-9a4d-5fb4-acdf-d84267e255c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "290\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135465/selection/93b4e4249d9b35bb6713c46b127a78989cfd6b42e3b3d296c2bb61576a72c06d" :answer/id #uuid "38ca66e9-278f-535f-8e35-8f246237a5ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "390\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135465/selection/128b46a7fe0ec567b8b1febe031b8cc9760d451f28573f98fdf7ea55f002059b" :answer/id #uuid "2c802ab6-a774-55a2-ba3a-78dca2c87b59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "340\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135465/selection/333fc25b8c6509f22487c4c0fa4e06a8286d9dd14248c969c19c84393b478be2" :answer/id #uuid "f837d30e-5eb2-51d0-a063-30752cd48247" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "240\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135465/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "237c83fd-10f6-5c94-9c97-1a878eb8b293" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-135465/selection/a9c70ee39590074bf42ecc3e38718cc08ac45916790a55fc58f014f0fb921912" :answer-field/choices ["q-135465/selection/704aec2380857c58272f76dc457192d5f0618fec55c4bc6c2c0c2c61accea4ea" "q-135465/selection/a9c70ee39590074bf42ecc3e38718cc08ac45916790a55fc58f014f0fb921912" "q-135465/selection/93b4e4249d9b35bb6713c46b127a78989cfd6b42e3b3d296c2bb61576a72c06d" "q-135465/selection/128b46a7fe0ec567b8b1febe031b8cc9760d451f28573f98fdf7ea55f002059b" "q-135465/selection/333fc25b8c6509f22487c4c0fa4e06a8286d9dd14248c969c19c84393b478be2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-135465" :question/id #uuid "04b428ea-6569-5051-b764-669659243c1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-135465" :question/problem "A certain company produces three types of ground coffee: fine, medium, and coarse. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of ground coffee each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Fine** | **Medium** | **Coarse** |\n| To: | **Fine** | $0.5$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n| **Medium** | $0.3$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ |  |\n| **Coarse** | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nFor example, $30%$ of fine ground coffee customers switch to medium ground coffee each year, and $20%$ of coarse ground coffee customers switch to fine ground coffee.\n\nCurrently, $300\\,000$ customers buy fine ground coffee, $500\\,000$ customers buy medium ground coffee, and $200\\,000$ customers buy coarse ground coffee. How many customers will purchase fine ground coffee in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.2 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of ground coffee is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}300\\,000 \\\\ 500\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}300\\,000 \\\\ 500\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.5 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.2 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.6 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.5 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ 0.47 \\\\ 0.24\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $29%$ of customers will buy fine ground coffee. Computing $29%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.29=290\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $290\\,000$ customers will buy fine ground coffee in one year." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-135465/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e93dde4-a8e8-548a-801e-3d9a320a4513"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-135465"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-135460/selection/f35a5c1d8a3a50269e7e724bb8f3da8d8b1fcfac8b4c51955889c05e5dfa2934" :answer/id #uuid "1466fc98-9779-59f9-9c03-f5c57361f58f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "360\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135460/selection/9381123a159cb977356a8c6c709ffb03e2392bde42ac7d1b054e44f8248d96a5" :answer/id #uuid "090808fe-f145-5375-bcba-cebf519f84ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "430\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135460/selection/eeb0f1ce394ca1b98393306fcae27d29a220cf376d6ca627a333d738054bbdf4" :answer/id #uuid "1b7d8e75-ef95-5123-8368-23d83857a25a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "280\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135460/selection/07910ec0e8dc98bccbf2b49f61ffef05f887c8ea4a8cef0e2f954f96b79097dc" :answer/id #uuid "f526a866-01da-5244-9b31-58ba7afe55ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "380\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135460/selection/010b45058f689266c69b500cfb43677150f3bace460e91902ba0a6b566dbcdb4" :answer/id #uuid "70b19f85-8219-5259-a797-6b720a7677fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "320\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135460/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f548f38-d37c-5a8d-8446-19cedf6297ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-135460/selection/f35a5c1d8a3a50269e7e724bb8f3da8d8b1fcfac8b4c51955889c05e5dfa2934" :answer-field/choices ["q-135460/selection/f35a5c1d8a3a50269e7e724bb8f3da8d8b1fcfac8b4c51955889c05e5dfa2934" "q-135460/selection/9381123a159cb977356a8c6c709ffb03e2392bde42ac7d1b054e44f8248d96a5" "q-135460/selection/eeb0f1ce394ca1b98393306fcae27d29a220cf376d6ca627a333d738054bbdf4" "q-135460/selection/07910ec0e8dc98bccbf2b49f61ffef05f887c8ea4a8cef0e2f954f96b79097dc" "q-135460/selection/010b45058f689266c69b500cfb43677150f3bace460e91902ba0a6b566dbcdb4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-135460" :question/id #uuid "c83a21c4-20b5-5d81-82df-2c0054d8944c" :question/math-academy-id "q-135460" :question/problem "A certain company produces three types of milk: whole, skim, and lactose-free. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of milk each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Whole** | **Skim** | **Lactose-free** |\n| To: | **Whole** | $0.5$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| **Skim** | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ |  |\n| **Lactose-free** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |  |\n\nFor example, $30%$ of whole milk customers switch to skim milk each year, and $20%$ of lactose-free milk customers switch to whole milk.\n\nCurrently, $400\\,000$ customers consume whole milk, $400\\,000$ customers consume skim milk, and $200\\,000$ customers consume lactose-free milk. How many customers will consume whole milk after one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of milk is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}400\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}400\\,000 \\\\ 400\\,000 \\\\ 200\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.4 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.3 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3 & 0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.4 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.36 \\\\ 0.36 \\\\ 0.28\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $36%$ of customers will consume whole milk. Computing $36%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.36=360\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $360\\,000$ customers will consume whole milk after one year." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-135460/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e93dde4-a8e8-548a-801e-3d9a320a4513"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-135460"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-135458/selection/86a098a766d72acf50dd7023f8dc1f95cb8811edb961e396b2bef09dec47b54b" :answer/id #uuid "e8b75824-49e3-5879-99a4-1b2847a7cc79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,800" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135458/selection/b790dfdecc2bfc3b1ae05753239ccc474f14e7ed56a390fb2aa7ac151e6fc5ec" :answer/id #uuid "f5fab232-a7bc-5b75-a91b-ec8e0cd8a3ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\,200" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135458/selection/73920dccb76ad5baa56aa1bb088f304182f6cab804ab5e44b13b3f30b37adea8" :answer/id #uuid "c780875d-94ba-5678-9842-a32cfbce6f6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\,100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135458/selection/1df6c78986db09194fedd65aec8d879adfd78a45d16bcb012ad224a0a8d91906" :answer/id #uuid "98ec2983-2653-562b-a96e-863ff082b2da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\,400" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135458/selection/a66880b8962b58bf2cfefd80e8fa173d0e1da74bffaebde312ff891d4e5d5d51" :answer/id #uuid "00566601-c5f6-5ca2-9cf6-d95445c8aa2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\,600" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135458/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d86fbd-2ba8-5a9e-af75-a6c013906351" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-135458/selection/b790dfdecc2bfc3b1ae05753239ccc474f14e7ed56a390fb2aa7ac151e6fc5ec" :answer-field/choices ["q-135458/selection/86a098a766d72acf50dd7023f8dc1f95cb8811edb961e396b2bef09dec47b54b" "q-135458/selection/b790dfdecc2bfc3b1ae05753239ccc474f14e7ed56a390fb2aa7ac151e6fc5ec" "q-135458/selection/73920dccb76ad5baa56aa1bb088f304182f6cab804ab5e44b13b3f30b37adea8" "q-135458/selection/1df6c78986db09194fedd65aec8d879adfd78a45d16bcb012ad224a0a8d91906" "q-135458/selection/a66880b8962b58bf2cfefd80e8fa173d0e1da74bffaebde312ff891d4e5d5d51"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-135458" :question/id #uuid "3bc34242-e133-5fba-9823-018a7dbf9dab" :question/math-academy-id "q-135458" :question/problem "A certain company produces two types of motorcycles: classic and high-cylinder. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of motorcycles each decade:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Classic** | **High-Cylinder** |\n| To: | **Classic** | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |\n| **High-Cylinder** | $0.4$ | $0.8$ |  |\n\nThat is, $40%$ of classic motorcycle customers switch to high-cylinder each decade, and $20%$ of high-cylinder motorcycle customers switch to Classic.\n\nCurrently, $7\\,000$ customers use classic motorcycles while $3\\,000$ customers use high-cylinder motorcycles. How many customers will use high-cylinder motorcycles in one decade? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of motorcycle is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7\\,000 \\\\ 3\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(10\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{10\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}7\\,000 \\\\ 3\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one decade, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.6 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.48 \\\\ 0.52\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one decade, $52%$ of customers will use high-cylinder motorcycles. Computing $52%$ of $10\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n10\\,000\\cdot 0.52=5\\,200.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5\\,200$ customers will use high-cylinder motorcycles in one decade." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-135458/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e93dde4-a8e8-548a-801e-3d9a320a4513"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-135458"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9162"] :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/70ee929c71d89a443d3ea0efc39ed225f344685059b86a04285a118a476da0f8.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{U}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0 \\\\ 1.2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{V}$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 0.95 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.05\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.35+0.65=1 \\\\ 0.95+0.05=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Matrix $\\bf{W}$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its third column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\bf{V}$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5598"] :question/problem "There are two groups of people in a particular city: those who use their own vehicles and those who use public transport. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport, and\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles.\n\nGive the stochastic matrix that describes how people change their means of transportation each year." :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each year: \n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport. This implies that $65\\%$ of them remain using their own vehicles.\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles. This implies that $75\\%$ of them remain using public transport.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Vehicles** | **Public Transport ** |\n| To: | **Vehicles** | $0.65$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Public Transport** | $0.35$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/b21fee1abe56dcfdc687be14278c62a20d201ff00e96f1e5918ce22d9dc8b03c.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5971"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/165917feacd28344502759fc6e03f96c5b54d9530701916b746fc489374a6d0b.png) \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector $\\mathbf{x}_0$ given above. Calculate the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_2,$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x_1}=P\\mathbf{x}_0, \\qquad \\mathbf{x}_2=P\\mathbf{x}_1\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.18 \\\\ 0.82\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.172 \\\\ 0.828\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5599"] :question/problem "A certain industry produces $2$ types of sugar, namely sugar $A$ and sugar $B$. The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of sugar in a month:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Sugar $A$** | **Sugar $B$** |\n| To: | **Sugar $A$** | $0.3$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sugar $B$** | $0.7$ | $0.55$ |  |\n\nThat is, $70\\%$ of sugar $A$ customers switch to sugar $B$ each month, and $45\\%$ of sugar $B$ customers switch to sugar $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600$ customers use sugar $A,$ while $400$ customers use sugar $B.$ How many customers will use sugar $A$ in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/451b82e8cb00ede7b0ddf73f35c0555d0eeeeefc5beb5ce4fddd54f0df5f2577.png)\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of sugar is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/56952aeed1d718033af8a3e6d09e8e269f1d70aa8dc31609b8289f025f2e6bd6.png)\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059948/assets/cae1bed6b8b01b3ff66c2e8bacf7a10f6c0ece53f3aeac0fb1a51e8fc3c07e8e.png)\n\nTo find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_1$ after one month, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1=P\\mathbf{x}_0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.55\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.36 \\\\ 0.64\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $\\color{blue}36\\%$ of customers will use sugar $A.$ Computing $36\\%$ of $1\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000 \\cdot 0.36 = 360.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $360$ customers will use sugar $A$ in one month." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
